# CODEOWNERS — REPO-BASELINE.md §1:
# "Commit a CODEOWNERS file that assigns reviewers to security-relevant paths (auth,
#  secrets handling, CI workflow definitions, infra/deploy scripts) BEFORE THE FIRST
#  FEATURE LANDS."
#
# The failure it prevents, in the table's own words: security-relevant paths get merged
# without the right reviewer.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# HOW THIS FILE IS READ
#
#   LAST MATCHING PATTERN WINS. Not the most specific one — the last one. So the global
#   default is FIRST and every narrowing rule is below it, and reordering this file
#   changes who reviews what. That is the single most common way a CODEOWNERS file ends
#   up meaning something other than it looks like it means.
#
#   Ownership is only ENFORCED if the branch protection rule on `main` has "Require
#   review from Code Owners" enabled. Without it this file is a notification list: the
#   right person is asked, and nothing stops a merge without them. That setting is a
#   repository setting and cannot be committed, which is worth knowing before assuming
#   this file is a gate.
#
#   A single-maintainer repository makes owner review advisory in practice — GitHub does
#   not ask an author to review their own pull request. The file is still worth having
#   for the day a second contributor arrives, and for what it DOCUMENTS: this is the list
#   of paths where a change is a security decision, written down where a reviewer sees it.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ── Global default ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Everything, unless a rule below overrides it.
